---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CapmFactorsModule from "../../components/CapmFactorsModule.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Risk & Return: CAPM & Factors"
  description="How markets price risk. Start with CAPM (one risk, the market's, measured by beta), then extend to the Fama–French factors, where size, value, profitability, and investment explain what CAPM called mysterious 'alpha.'"
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>How markets price risk</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Diversification leaves one risk you can't escape: the market's. The
      <strong>Capital Asset Pricing Model</strong> says that's the only risk you're
      paid for, and prices every asset by a single number: its beta. But decades of
      data showed beta wasn't the whole story, so Fama and French added more
      <strong>factors</strong>. Meet both, in order: one risk, then many.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <CapmFactorsModule client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>CAPM: the price of market risk</h2>
      <p>
        Once you've diversified away company-specific risk, what's left is exposure
        to the market itself, captured by <strong>beta</strong>. CAPM says an
        asset's fair expected return is just the risk-free rate plus its beta times
        the market's risk premium. Nothing else should be rewarded. Measure beta
        from a scatter of returns, and it sets the fair return on the Security Market
        Line.
      </p>
      <h2>Factors: beyond beta</h2>
      <p>
        In the data, small stocks, cheap "value" stocks, and profitable, conservative
        firms all earned <em>more</em> than their beta could explain. Fama and French
        turned those persistent patterns into extra <strong>factors</strong>
        (size, value, profitability, investment), and what CAPM called mysterious
        "alpha" became exposure you can dial on purpose. The lesson isn't that CAPM
        was wrong, but that "the market" is one of several kinds of compensated risk.
        Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="factors" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/factors" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure-wide);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>
